Factorise the denominator and perform partial fraction expansion: \[H(s)=\frac{s+3}{s^2+2s+1}
=\frac{s+3}{(s+1)^2}.\] Let \[\frac{s+3}{(s+1)^2}
=\frac{A}{s+1}+\frac{B}{(s+1)^2}.\] Multiplying by \((s+1)^2\), \[s+3=A(s+1)+B.\] Comparing coefficients, \[A=1,\qquad A+B=3 \;\Rightarrow\; B=2.\] Hence, \[H(s)=\frac{1}{s+1}+\frac{2}{(s+1)^2}.\] Using standard inverse Laplace transforms, \[\mathcal{L}^{-1}\left\{\frac{1}{s+a}\right\}=e^{-at},\] \[\mathcal{L}^{-1}\left\{\frac{1}{(s+a)^2}\right\}=te^{-at}.\] Therefore, \[h(t)=e^{-t}+2te^{-t}, \qquad t\ge0.\] \[\boxed{\text{(d) }2te^{-t}+e^{-t}}\]

Given \[H(s)=\frac{a_{1}s^{2}+b_{1}s+c_{1}}{a_{2}s^{2}+b_{2}s+c_{2}},\] with \[a_{1}=0,\qquad b_{1}=0.\] Hence, \[H(s)=\frac{c_{1}}{a_{2}s^{2}+b_{2}s+c_{2}}.\] Evaluate the gain at the extreme frequencies: At DC \((s=0)\), \[H(0)=\frac{c_{1}}{c_{2}}\neq 0.\] As frequency increases \((s=j\omega,\ \omega\rightarrow\infty)\), \[|H(j\omega)|\approx
\frac{c_{1}}{a_{2}\omega^{2}}
\longrightarrow 0.\] Thus, the system passes low frequencies and attenuates high frequencies. Hence, it is a low-pass filter. \[\boxed{\text{(d) Low pass filter}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>A low-pass filter has finite gain at \(\omega=0\) and zero gain as \(\omega\rightarrow\infty\).</li><li>Compare the highest powers of \(s\) in the numerator and denominator to determine the high-frequency behavior.</li><li>A constant numerator indicates attenuation of high-frequency components.</li></ul></div>

