The transfer function of the phase lead compensator is \[D(s)=3\left(\dfrac{s+\dfrac{1}{3T}}{s+\dfrac{1}{T}}\right)=\dfrac{1+s(3T)}{1+sT}\] Comparing with the standard form \[D(s)=K_c\left(\frac{1+sT_z}{1+sT_p}\right)\] we get \[K_c=1,\qquad T_z=3T,\qquad T_p=T\] For a phase lead compensator, the frequency at which the phase is maximum is \[\omega_m=\frac{1}{\sqrt{T_zT_p}}\] Therefore, \[\omega_m=\frac{1}{\sqrt{(3T)(T)}}
=\frac{1}{\sqrt{3T^2}}
=\sqrt{\frac{1}{3T^2}}\] \[\boxed{(B)\;\sqrt{\frac{1}{3T^2}}\ \text{rad/s}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For a phase lead compensator, the frequency corresponding to maximum phase is \(\omega_m=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{T_zT_p}}\)</li><li>Always convert the compensator into the standard form \(K_c\dfrac{1+sT_z}{1+sT_p}\) before applying the formula.</li></ul></div>

