Numbers to arrange: \(\{10,\; 7,\; 5,\; 4,\; 2\}\). Apply the three constraints simultaneously: <ol><li>No two odd or even numbers may be adjacent (must alternate odd–even or even–odd).</li><li>Second number from left \(= \dfrac{1}{2} \times\) leftmost number.</li><li>Middle number \(= 2 \times\) rightmost number.</li></ol> <strong>Odd numbers:</strong> \(\{7, 5\}\); <strong>Even numbers:</strong> \(\{10, 4, 2\}\). From constraint 2: leftmost is even (so its half is an integer in the set). Testing leftmost \(= 10\): second \(= 5\) (, odd, alternates). From constraint 3: middle \(= 2 \times\) rightmost. Testing rightmost \(= 2\): middle \(= 4\) (, even). Remaining positions: slots 4 and 5 hold \(\{7, 2\}\). Rightmost \(= 2\) (even); slot 4 \(= 7\) (odd) — alternation maintained. \[\underbrace{10}_{\text{pos 1}}\;\;
    \underbrace{5}_{\text{pos 2}}\;\;
    \underbrace{4}_{\text{pos 3 (middle)}}\;\;
    \underbrace{7}_{\text{pos 4}}\;\;
    \underbrace{2}_{\text{pos 5 (rightmost)}}\] Second from the right \(=\) position 4 \(= 7\). \[\boxed{\text{(c) 7}}\]

Both trains start at 7,AM from the same point and travel in <strong>opposite directions</strong>, so their speeds add up (relative speed concept). \[v_{1} = 80\,\text{km/h}\;(\text{North}), \qquad
    v_{2} = 100\,\text{km/h}\;(\text{South})\] \[v_{\text{rel}} = v_{1} + v_{2} = 80 + 100 = 180\,\text{km/h}\] \[\text{Distance between them} = 540\,\text{km}\] \[\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{v_{\text{rel}}}
               = \frac{540}{180} = 3\,\text{hours}\] \[\text{Time of day} = 7\,\text{AM} + 3\,\text{h} = 10\,\text{AM}\] \[\boxed{\text{(B) 10}}\]

\[\text{Machine } X \text{ completes the order in } 4\,\text{hours}
    \implies \text{rate of } X = \frac{1}{4}\,\text{order/hour}\] \[\text{Machine } Y \text{ completes the order in } 2\,\text{hours}
    \implies \text{rate of } Y = \frac{1}{2}\,\text{order/hour}\] Combined rate when both work simultaneously: \[\text{Rate}_{X+Y} = \frac{1}{4} + \frac{1}{2}
                      = \frac{1}{4} + \frac{2}{4}
                      = \frac{3}{4}\,\text{order/hour}\] Time to complete one full order together: \[T = \frac{1}{\dfrac{3}{4}} = \frac{4}{3}\,\text{hours}\] \[\boxed{C )\dfrac {4}{3}}\]

The hour hand moves \(360^{\circ}\) in 12 hours \(= 0.5^{\circ}\) per minute, or equivalently \(30^{\circ}\) per hour. So \(30^{\circ} \equiv 1\,\text{hour}\). <strong>Gross hours before breaks:</strong><br/>\[P\text{ stopped at }210^{\circ} \implies T_{P} = \frac{210}{30} = 7\,\text{hours}\] \[Q\text{ stopped at }240^{\circ} \implies T_{Q} = \frac{240}{30} = 8\,\text{hours}\] <strong>Deducting unpaid breaks:</strong><br/><ul><li>\(P\): two tea breaks of 15,min each \(= 30\,\text{min}\); no lunch. \(\implies\) Paid hours \(= 7\,\text{h} - 0.5\,\text{h} = 6.5\,\text{h}\)</li><li>\(Q\): one lunch break of 20,min; no tea breaks. \(\implies\) Paid hours \(= 8\,\text{h} - \dfrac{1}{3}\,\text{h}
        = \dfrac{23}{3}\,\text{h}\)</li></ul> <strong>Total paid hours:</strong><br/>\[T_{\text{total}} = \frac{13}{2} + \frac{23}{3}
                   = \frac{39}{6} + \frac{46}{6}
                   = \frac{85}{6}\,\text{hours}\] <strong>Total payment</strong> at USD,200 per hour: \[\text{Payment} = \frac{85}{6} \times 200 = \frac{17000}{6}
                 \approx \text{USD}\;2833.33\] <strong>Remaining budget:</strong><br/>\[\text{Remaining} = 3000 - 2833.33 = \text{USD}\;166.67\] \[\boxed{ \text B) 166.67}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Hour-hand conversion: \(30^{\circ} = 1\,\text{hour}\) (since \(360^{\circ} \div 12\,\text{h} = 30^{\circ}/\text{h}\)).</li><li>Only <strong>working time minus break time</strong> is billable; add all consultants' paid hours before multiplying by the rate.</li></ul></div>

Sets: \(X = \{1,\;2,\;3\}\), \(Y = \{2,\;3,\;4\}\). Every element of \(Z\) has the form \(Z = \dfrac{x}{y}\) where \(x \in X\), \(y \in Y\). <strong>Minimum element</strong> of \(Z\): smallest numerator, largest denominator: \[Z_{\min} = \frac{X_{\min}}{Y_{\max}} = \frac{1}{4}\] <strong>Maximum element</strong> of \(Z\): largest numerator, smallest denominator: \[Z_{\max} = \frac{X_{\max}}{Y_{\min}} = \frac{3}{2}\] Product of minimum and maximum elements: \[Z_{\min} \times Z_{\max}
    = \frac{1}{4} \times \frac{3}{2}
    = \frac{3}{8}\] \[\boxed{\text{D) }\dfrac{3}{8}}\]

Let initial populations of states X and Y be \(a\) and \(b\) respectively. Initial ratio: \[P = \frac{a}{b}\] After increases of \(x\%\) and \(y\%\): \[P' = \frac{a\!\left(1 + \dfrac{x}{100}\right)}{b\!\left(1 + \dfrac{y}{100}\right)}
       = \frac{a}{b} \cdot \frac{100 + x}{100 + y}\] Percentage change in \(P\): \[\frac{P' - P}{P} \times 100
    = \left[\frac{\dfrac{a}{b}\cdot\dfrac{100+x}{100+y} - \dfrac{a}{b}}
                 {\dfrac{a}{b}}\right] \times 100
    = \frac{100(x - y)}{100 + y}\] \[\boxed{\text{D) }\dfrac{100(x-y)}{100+y}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Express the new ratio in terms of the old ratio, then compute \(\dfrac{P'-P}{P}\times100\) — the \(\dfrac{a}{b}\) cancels cleanly.</li><li>Since \(x &gt; y\), the numerator \(100(x-y) &gt; 0\), confirming \(P\) increases.</li></ul></div>

Let pipe \(A\) (Y in question) fill in 4,h, pipe \(B\) (X in question) fill in 5,h, and outlet pipe \(C\) drain in 20,h. Net rate when all three operate simultaneously: \[\text{Net rate} = \frac{1}{4} + \frac{1}{5} - \frac{1}{20}
                    = \frac{5}{20} + \frac{4}{20} - \frac{1}{20}
                    = \frac{8}{20} = \frac{2}{5}\,\text{tank/hour}\] Time to fill the tank: \[T = \frac{1}{\,\dfrac{2}{5}\,} = \frac{5}{2} = 2.5\,\text{hours}\] \[\boxed{\text{C) 2.50}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Net rate \(=\) (sum of inlet rates) \(-\) (sum of outlet rates).</li><li>Time \(= \dfrac{1}{\text{Net rate}}\).</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do <strong>not</strong> ignore the drainage pipe; failing to subtract its rate leads to an overly optimistic (shorter) fill time.</li></ul></div>
